    "result_text": "All 4 tests pass and the build succeeds, including the new `coreRejectionIsProblemJson` acceptance test.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change requires every rejection response (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. Previously these handlers returned a plain-JSON `ValidationErrorsResponse` (`{\"errors\":[...]}`).\n\n**What I did** \u2014 all in `ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`, which is where HTTP-status mapping belongs:\n\n- Added one shared private helper, `fieldRejection(e, status, request, detail, fields)`, that builds a `ProblemDetail` via the existing `detailBuild(...)` (so it inherits the same `type`/`title`/`status`/`detail`/`timestamp` structure as the 400/404/500 handlers), attaches the offending field names as an `errors` extension member, and wraps it in a `ResponseEntity`. Spring MVC serializes a `ProblemDetail` body as `application/problem+json` automatically \u2014 the same mechanism the pre-existing 500 handler relied on.\n- Converted all ten rejection handlers to delegate to that helper: the `MethodArgumentNotValidException` missing-fields branch (400), `MissingOwnerFieldsException` (400), the four single-field 400 handlers (telephone, email, disposable-domain, postcode, future-date), the three 409 handlers (duplicate identity, household duplicate, city limit), and the 429 daily-limit handler. Each now takes `HttpServletRequest` so the problem `type` URI can be derived, and each preserves its distinct debug logging.\n- Deleted the now-unused `ValidationErrorsResponse` record (verified no remaining references in `src/main` or `src/test`).\n\nHTTP status codes are unchanged; the offending-field information is retained (moved into the problem body's `errors` member) rather than dropped. No rejection responses are produced outside this advice \u2014 the controllers only emit 200/201/204/404 directly \u2014 so this single point of change covers the whole requirement.",
